# Backend

## Layering

Routes speak HTTP; services speak domain. SQL and orchestration stay out of route handlers. Pydantic schemas define the wire contract; SQLAlchemy models define persistence.

## Access and collaboration

Authorization is centralized—check membership and role through shared access helpers, then enforce at the boundary. Project-wide and topic-scoped membership are different concepts; model and check the right one.

Reuse existing invite/member endpoints when they already match the product intent; add tables only when scope truly differs.

## Data and schema

Prefer JSONB metadata over sprouting nullable columns for ad-hoc tags. Registry rows in `rag.*` must stay aligned with app UUIDs for projects and files—register on create, don’t orphan chunks.

Schema changes ride Alembic with a single head. Run from `backend/` via Poetry (`poetry run alembic …`); production migrates inside the `app` container.

## Integrations in code

LLM and embeddings go through the gateway client module—nowhere else. Long-running or fan-out work belongs on the worker, not inside WebSocket handlers or request paths that users wait on.

## Module hygiene

Avoid name collisions between framework types and ORM models (import aliases at boundaries). Break circular imports by extracting shared primitives into a neutral module rather than lazy-import gymnastics.

New env knobs: `Settings` + `.env.example`, documented in place.

## Before you finish

If the change touches AI, chat, or retrieval, confirm one shared context-builder path feeds all of them—not parallel prompt/RAG assembly.
